---
import { massWellReach, STANDARD_SUN } from "@void-drift/core";
import { GRAVITY_TUNING } from "@void-drift/mode-a";
import SunFlightBench from "../../../components/lab/SunFlightBench.svelte";
import LabLayout from "../../../layouts/LabLayout.astro";

const reach = massWellReach(
  GRAVITY_TUNING.sunMass,
  GRAVITY_TUNING.cutoff,
  GRAVITY_TUNING.falloff,
);
---

<LabLayout title="Suns" wide>
  <article>
    <p class="eyebrow">WORLD / STANDARD SUN</p>
    <h1>A bargain inside the well</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The standard Sun is a steady G star: a familiar source of energy, a
      gravitational commitment and a lethal body. Approach to collect. Leave
      before the bargain becomes an impact.
    </p>
    <h2>A star at the scale of a flight</h2>
    <p>
      Temperature, radius, mass and radiant output describe different properties
      of a star. Void Drift compresses their distances and times into an
      encounter a pilot can read. The luminous body is a terminal boundary, not
      solid ground. Fictional shielding lets a ship survive brief exposure
      nearby; it does not make the Sun traversable. Decorative rings are
      atmosphere, not precise safety markers.
    </p>
    <h2>Three overlapping fields</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Zone, from the centre</th>
          <th>What happens</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Surface · {STANDARD_SUN.radius} u</td>
          <td>
            Contact destroys the ship. Existing ship collision geometry remains
            in force.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Shoulder · to {STANDARD_SUN.shoulder} u</td>
          <td>
            The 20 u shoulder beyond the surface supplies 7.5 E/s per
            collector-equivalent and 1.5 hull/s exposure. Close collection is
            never a promise of safe clearance.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Hazard · to {STANDARD_SUN.damageReach} u</td>
          <td>
            Beyond the shoulder, exposure declines linearly to zero at 100 u.
            Outside this envelope there is no Sun proximity damage.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Collection and gravity · {reach.toFixed(2)} u</td>
          <td>
            Collection declines linearly from 7.5 E/s at 55 u to 0.75 E/s at the
            boundary, then stops outside it. Gravity keeps its existing force
            law and cutoff.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      There is room to collect outside the damaging envelope, but still inside
      the gravitational well. An inward pass builds speed and bends the path.
      The outward climb still needs an exit plan; neither a green line nor
      positive income guarantees escape.
    </p>
    <h2>Every ship can collect</h2>
    <p>
      One collector-equivalent is inherent in every ship. It takes no slot,
      fitting or unlock. Each fitted solar collector adds another equivalent;
      two equivalents double gross income at every distance. Gravity collection
      is a separate fitted capability and does not replace this baseline.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Wasp is the reference: life support draws 0.8 E/s and its single Light
      ion draws 1.0 E/s when fully firing. Steering stabilizers also consume
      power when used. Other engine families retain their own costs. At the
      outer edge, 0.75 E/s cannot cover even life support: collection has begun
      while the battery still drains. Flying inward improves income, with
      full-thrust break-even around 282 u for the unsteered Wasp.
    </p>
    <h2>Stored energy is a real limit</h2>
    <p>
      A battery holds its actual fitted capacity. Collection above capacity is
      wasted; it does not build a hidden reserve. Engine delivery falls with
      available energy, and exhausted reserves end a Survival run. Hull damage
      and contact remain separate failure paths. Practice flight retains its
      deliberate resource protection, so use Survival or the live reference
      below to assess depletion. No repair device or automatic hull recovery is
      included.
    </p>
    <h2>Read the field, then read the ship</h2>
    <p>
      Dim dashed Android green marks gravity. Solid green marks collection: it
      brightens with actual collection and gains a 3.3% green fill. The hazard
      envelope is dotted amber until Sun damage is applied, then dotted red with
      an 11% red fill. Active collection describes gross supply, even at a full
      battery or while consumption exceeds income.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every boundary stroke stays one CSS pixel wide across zoom and display
      density. Where the two outer boundaries coincide, the solar line is drawn
      two CSS pixels inward so both remain visible. This is a display offset;
      both physical reaches stay identical.
    </p>
    <SunFlightBench client:load />
    <h2>Explore the budget without changing the flight</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/lab/instrument">Motion instrument</a> and
      <a href="/lab/gravity-tuning">Gravity tuning</a>
      share optional collection-field configuration: centre, independent reach,
      shoulder, peak and outer fraction. Their energy view shows collection,
      consumption, net power and cumulative battery change on one time cursor.
      Those comparisons explicitly exclude energy feedback, capacity and
      starvation; their battery change is an unclamped energy integral, not
      stored charge.
    </p>
    <p>
      The
      <a href="/lab/studies/suns-2026-11-07">accepted design study</a>
      preserves earlier alternatives and forced HUD comparisons as experiments.
      This article describes the implemented standard. Other stellar families,
      Sun artwork and effect redesign remain deferred. Automated checks
      establish mechanics; human flight review still judges the bargain’s feel.
    </p>
  </article>
</LabLayout>
<style>
article {
  max-width: 1100px;
  padding: 40px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  line-height: 1.65;
}
h1 {
  font-size: 40px;
  line-height: 1.15;
}
h2 {
  margin-top: 40px;
}
.lead {
  font-size: 22px;
  max-width: 850px;
}
.eyebrow {
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
}
table {
  border-collapse: collapse;
  width: 100%;
}
th,
td {
  padding: 16px;
  text-align: left;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  vertical-align: top;
}
th:first-child,
td:first-child {
  width: 30%;
}
a {
  color: var(--color-text);
}
</style>
